\pard\plain \ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Ringing pierced Tex\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s ears with a strange bubbling static. It wasn\u8217\'92t a normal ringing but an alien tinnitus he had never felt before. It was as though he had fallen into a pool, but without the press of the waters pressure against him. Sound felt weightless, in the sense that he could not feel percussion, it was just there, untouchable, but present. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0  }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 It was like his ears were being born into existence for the first time, hearing the first sounds he had ever heard. There was something entirely off. Like he was no longer a part of his own body, or any body. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 All he could see was white emptiness. Eventually the ringing stopped, There was no taste, smell or feeling of weight. He felt senseless, free of the body, but he still had a conscience. He could still feel the pain brought on by memories, all his past trauma, but only in the mind. He had no breath or pulse. He drifted in the minds construct, and in this while he felt in control, he could not help but feel like he was at the complete mercy of whatever this was. He embraced the silence, trusting the process. It was the first peaceful moment he had felt in what seemed like an eternity. Nothing could harm him here other than his thoughts, his very being was that of his own }{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "scrivcmt://6DF4FD00-F356-461B-9014-BF087C1AC186"}{\fldrslt\f0\fs24\b0\i0 will.}}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Then the silence stopped. He could feel again. He was falling, battling against a cold piercing wind that stormed against his face.  All sense of feeling came back to him, right until before his body floated ever so slowly through the roof of a small  seemingly insignificant container secluded, full and univiting. This container while in his mind he knew it seemed inconvenient, he was revisiting an old place he was all too familiar with. The pilot containers of the cleanup crew called in after the war. He remembered everything as though he had just breathed the cold air, and flet hte mist of his breath fog the screen which emmitted artificial light thorugh  the dark void surrounding him.  he recalled the feeling of the cold joystick when he grasped it, st}{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "scrivcmt://8D37B5D5-0C65-45A1-85B3-6127BE70239E"}{\fldrslt\f0\fs24\b0\i0 rangely no matter how long he held onto that joystick controlling the machines, scraping the remnants of a history and culture he could only grasp at in a distant lifetime. No longer how long he held the joy-stick, it was cold and heavy. It didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t matter how much debris he cleared from that container, He could not help but feel like he was berrying a part of himself in that strange foreign land. And while the news spoke of remembrance, it was he who bared the images, the burnt flesh, the artifacts and cherrished belongings, berried deep beneath a soil now only able to bear not life, but the archi}}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 ves of a generation he would never know. In his sleep they came back to him, for many years to come. He wandered on in his dreams for what felt like eternity. When he woke he would read poetry, wonder if his children would feel the weight on his breath, the strangling fold of mankinds injustice. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0  . His stomach, or whatever he flet of it was all but gone. What lifht would shine through the small window of htat container was always jaded by an overcast cloud. Although the cloud was not real, it was a cloud of doubt in the mission which h ewas fighting for. The boards beneath him felt thin. He like he could fall through at any moment. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
