\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f1\fs20\b0\i0 I have been dreaming allot lately. Last night I dreamt of her, my x. I feel sad to think of her. The dream felt convicting, as we walked into a great hall, but this hall was different to a normal court. It was an open area and instead of rows. The judge sat atop a podium looking down over all those who entered in single file along a white proud and gloss coated fence. The white fence gleaned and slithered it\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\af1\uc1\u8217\'92s way around green well kept greens of medium length grass, soft to the touch, forgiving more than the onwatchers to those who lost balance and fell. The whole room felt like a balancing act where those around you watched on as you tried to keep balance while paying attention to the front. It felt likeby holding hands you may become more interlinked in this position. I kept losing my balance, once I had found my place. The sad part in this passage is that at one point when I felt I had found my place, we were all looking around at one another. Below the Judges stand, there was a row looking back at us, stood atop their own white fence with smooth rails.. It was in this moment, when I made eye contact with her,  that I felt just how awkward the rail of teh fence I was standing on felt. The rail was not flat so that one could keep balance with ease. It was curved, depriving the heel any grounding you perched atop on your souls finding balance on you toes. If you leant back to far on your heels you would soon meet the ground below you. While I made contact with her, she was smiling, not looking at me. It was as though I could see her but I was completely invisible to her. The person next to her smiled aswell, as they exchanged glances between one another. I wanted to be him, smiling with her. I felt my balance begin to tip, and eventually I fell back, ripping the rail off with me. I was confused to find the rail had been attached by nails instead of screws. Perhaps if it had been fastened with screws, It may have caught my fall as I reached with my feet underneath it to correct my balance. But it only gave way. I felt conflicted between embarrassment and frustration. Once I found my position again. I found her again with my eyes, still she hadn\u8217\'92t noticed me. I edged closer along the rails, trying to get a closer glance at her. As I got closer I slowly made out her jacket, a dark navy blue with a dark glossed emblem faint by the angle but the light that licked it pronounced the emblemes distinct features. She was wearing a police sweater, and for some reason this made me happy, like she had found a place among a group, but I felt even further away from her not being a part of it. She had moved on with a new life without me. And the longer I waited the further awayay she went. I don\u8217\'92t know why I feel so dead inside after this dream. Perhaps it si me still trying to let go I have a partner now, times have been uncertain for us. Perhaps it is in the times of uncertainty we often look back on past relationships at what once felt grounded. Admittedly Mya was a very grounded person, one of her strenghts was her persistance and stubbornness to fight not only for herself but her family back home. This amounted an unwavering dedication within her, that surpassed any urge to give up when confronted with 3rd world issues like getting along with her peers. The terrible strength that suffering bestows upon people. I feel guilty in a sense like I abandoned her. I did abandon her. Thats hard to live with at times . And makes it even more convicting. The open court of pasture I feel is a place where I am happy to be in commonality with others, but a part of it is confronting. It is symbolic of how far we have come and are yet to come in our shared capacity. But the uncertainty in all the beauty comes from our struggle to find balance within ourselves. The nails feel deliberate in thes sense that we must not rely on our footing to hold us up, but our own feet instead. It is not just our body but our mind that keeps the balance, if one should fail, we cannot expect the ground beneath us to save us, but instead find grounding in our mind first.  }
